#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171767 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "2b0e57b0-a667-5bb2-bb1e-69c4d39b799e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8474" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,1]?$\n\n- $p: \\{ 0, 1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $q: \\mathbb{R} \\to [ 0, 1 ]$\n\n- $r: [0,1] \\to [ 0, 1 )$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f: X \\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $\\mathbb{R},$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[ 0, 1 ],$ the interval from $0$ and $1$ (including $0$ and $1$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $[ 0, 1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (including $0$ but not including $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171768 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "686f5d13-dfe9-5434-abff-052c7a4b76ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8475" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$.\"*\n\n- $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\n- $g: Y \\to \\mathbb{Q}$\n\n- $g:\\mathbb{R} \\to Y$" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $\\mathbb Q,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171769 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/id #uuid "c02bc2d0-fccb-5fbe-8914-c42ebc7bb9fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8476" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-x^3?$\n\n- $g:x \\mapsto x-x^3$\n\n- $g:x \\mapsto -x^3$\n\n- $g:-x \\mapsto x^3$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $g(x)=x-x^3$ means that the function $g$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x-x^3$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\ng:x \\mapsto x-x^3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171770 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/id #uuid "086ea590-785a-539d-8b0f-b69ac7301ce4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8487" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$\n\n- The codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- The range of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$ to the set $\\mathbb{R}.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\},$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}.$ Note that this is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4128809-363c-51c1-8592-9f3936fee831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302278} {:answer/id #uuid "5227df3f-ab60-55c7-8ac0-fe9bd93c6a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302279} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4c0902-125d-54ff-84ad-a0c0be98ff01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302280} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b2a44f-9cde-5878-9ef7-9bcd35563794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302281} {:answer/id #uuid "e110387c-5d06-579a-8c09-819eb852b13f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e110387c-5d06-579a-8c09-819eb852b13f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302282} :answer-field/id #uuid "6611876c-4df0-5aa8-80a6-e2e64a557ce0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f04f32d-b52c-5a8d-8938-51184b8d7e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-104778" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain ${0,1}?$\n\n- $p:{0,1}\\to Z$\n- $q:[0,1]\\to {0,1}$\n- $r:{0,1}\\to (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is ${0,1},$ the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only.\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $(0,1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (excluding both $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2903990c-fd4a-5d91-8ede-4de1d7bc5151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302285} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ed7ee9-7247-5e2f-9f96-ea40fceed33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302286} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8b4596-b747-5cfe-907a-09e801147241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302287} {:answer/id #uuid "2adf4bca-529f-5c5d-a0a8-1f0a2c2620f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302288} {:answer/id #uuid "03267076-3d3b-5fbe-abb4-e49ff47e30ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d8b4596-b747-5cfe-907a-09e801147241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302287} :answer-field/id #uuid "b23f50a5-ebb6-5140-b58f-738dce4e9b7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79ad1836-637c-5f60-ba4b-8156899fd58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104780" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain $Z?$\n\n- $p:Z\\to [0,3]$\n- $q:(0,7)\\to Z$\n- $r:{Z}^{+}\\to Z$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is $Z,$ the set of integers.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $(0,7),$ the interval from $0$ to $7$ (excluding $0$ and $7$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is ${Z}^{+},$ the set of positive integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0aa8b25-3875-502a-8844-a582df18a26a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Z\\to Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302292} {:answer/id #uuid "d12bb5bd-79d7-5615-bebc-166f8f14cf1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Z\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302293} {:answer/id #uuid "f89a6e46-3aa2-5135-8be2-70c4d9f02e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:R\\to Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302294} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb74ba8-37d6-51e8-9c53-62635334722b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Z\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302295} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ea2a66-f2a4-523e-abd7-65a1ca269bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Q\\to Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d12bb5bd-79d7-5615-bebc-166f8f14cf1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Z\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302293} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cd4afdf-94c9-5b3f-8552-4e9992ec36fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bca920a4-0d43-5e7e-8086-ae27453a33a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104845" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set of integers to the set of rational numbers.\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of integers to the set of rational numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:Z\\to Q$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $Z,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Q.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7752ef8-8d2e-5e40-89fc-a489779f48cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=2x-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302299} {:answer/id #uuid "5e354bd4-43c2-5272-9421-499b04987008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302300} {:answer/id #uuid "3e649bc9-a820-53cb-8185-61a02176dda4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=2-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302301} {:answer/id #uuid "5757c146-7dc0-5ee5-ac9d-37ebd3c05f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2x-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302302} {:answer/id #uuid "4e89b095-e886-589b-a26e-ae05f9fe018f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(2x)=-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7752ef8-8d2e-5e40-89fc-a489779f48cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=2x-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302299} :answer-field/id #uuid "573a217a-9a18-522d-85ec-951afb31960b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe7741f5-845f-50c6-819b-272e9ed7dddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-104938" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $f:x↦2x-{x}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:x↦2x-{x}^{3}$ means that the function $f$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $2x-{x}^{3}$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-{x}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b67bad6-8462-5909-b7b7-5f5c78d12453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)=x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302306} {:answer/id #uuid "a755afef-88cd-556b-a437-2163b5ab04df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302307} {:answer/id #uuid "703ff5f0-aa44-55fd-96d3-146127a08c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302308} {:answer/id #uuid "333fdd93-abae-5c1e-8897-5c3c3589f75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h({x}^{2})=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302309} {:answer/id #uuid "83be9039-8a3a-5a06-9bc0-7cbffe0bb597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)={x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83be9039-8a3a-5a06-9bc0-7cbffe0bb597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)={x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302310} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a45df6b-5cf6-5b77-9824-97229fa56b70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb3c7872-b3f4-59ad-9a6e-68449cf7368e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104940" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $h:x↦{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $h:x↦{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ means that the function $h$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to ${x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84d56587-b9f1-5925-8553-87fd754032fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302313} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6f626a-0dca-50ee-8f7c-3d27f689b21b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302314} {:answer/id #uuid "3c51ff3f-f86b-577a-8f7f-3e18a7f347b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302315} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c7b570-512e-52f2-9898-f2a7f7e028d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302316} {:answer/id #uuid "c65cd95f-6745-5713-90e8-92e938d30cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c65cd95f-6745-5713-90e8-92e938d30cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302317} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc768907-8b1f-5e3e-8f7e-3d211b07f167" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7782f49b-6c7d-5cc6-8bc2-01016218b1c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-104945" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g:{0,1}\\to Z?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is ${0,1}$\n- The codomain of $g$ is $Z$\n- The range of $g$ is ${0,1}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng:{0,1}\\to Z\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set ${0,1}$ to the set $Z.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is ${0,1},$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Z.$ Note that ${0,1}⊆Z$ is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10831a1b-999d-56dc-831b-2ea6d0ecf1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302320} {:answer/id #uuid "2585c9a8-9e83-5943-a8e7-c052a5d0ff57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302321} {:answer/id #uuid "098ee2ea-a5a7-51fe-96d8-cc258b0ea243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302322} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2acc40-be97-5657-89dc-4959bdf8230b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302323} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a62494-1636-5846-8b9b-a048932e39e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3a62494-1636-5846-8b9b-a048932e39e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302324} :answer-field/id #uuid "78f14f18-6b7a-566f-b335-004ee8c31295" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09774502-a6ed-5ac8-b6fb-e517d1f4974f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104946" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g:Q\\to R?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is $R$\n- The codomain of $g$ is $R$\n- The range of $g$ is $Q$" :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng:Q\\to R\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $Q$ to the set $R.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $Q,$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $R.$ Note that $Q⊂R$ is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9377612-74f1-536a-918e-4f4b46a21318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302327} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e18915-94d8-59b8-89e4-e2d79544abdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302328} {:answer/id #uuid "595728f8-d9c7-54c9-8ee3-394724a9d9e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302329} {:answer/id #uuid "727cb258-0e75-5854-a03b-a05f2a7ebcc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302330} {:answer/id #uuid "671b81a7-4920-5158-8701-ed322c54c773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "595728f8-d9c7-54c9-8ee3-394724a9d9e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302329} :answer-field/id #uuid "0db130e5-b590-5d33-b222-c1521597c1f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44c659b0-02a0-5558-84be-8148a9610549" :question/math-academy-id "q-104954" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/assets/8ff01afa1993f8e301edb69e3e7a13ee71ab01b1a3da416aa53ee94d021184e1.png)\n\nConsider the function $g:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$\n- The codomain of $g$ is ${-1,0,1,2}$\n- The range of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $g:X\\to Y$ means that the function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $g$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X={-1,0,1,2},$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y={0,1,2},$\n\n- the range of $g$ is ${0,1,2},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12756fea-d511-5413-ba42-e73f9ba0c031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: Q → X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171290} {:answer/id #uuid "2a18c8bc-4ef6-50fd-a7fb-41e818cc22c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: X → R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171291} {:answer/id #uuid "3809dc43-4135-5464-91b6-25b44f1ab772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: R → R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171292} {:answer/id #uuid "43760b88-aeaa-5fdb-b186-992ef13e2b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f: X → Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171293} {:answer/id #uuid "59cf8c2b-0ce0-5a3c-a0a7-b3fdd9e769b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: R → X" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a18c8bc-4ef6-50fd-a7fb-41e818cc22c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g: X → R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171291} :answer-field/id #uuid "920e367e-2ddb-534f-b346-fd7c372957ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25726fd8-78e9-529e-acdf-5c4c5a237b40" :question/math-academy-id "q-96040" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set of real numbers.\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set of real numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:X\\to R$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $X,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5b61f85-3fb5-5e7a-ae7c-2c21aa5416b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) = x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171296} {:answer/id #uuid "c96bbfb9-417a-5006-b749-44e919e111b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x^{2}) = x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171297} {:answer/id #uuid "cb07c416-f9f0-5b46-9ddb-a5e7898d48e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = x^{2} - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171298} {:answer/id #uuid "306f5c46-2f44-52dc-a4fa-f83548aa45cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171299} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd986ce-2e45-53f6-b615-bcea6906bd24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) = x^{2} - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd986ce-2e45-53f6-b615-bcea6906bd24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x) = x^{2} - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171300} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef1b2243-87a7-5305-864f-204dd2a1a022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97c5cfab-2044-5c8a-b60f-8b5878680b6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96042" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $f:x↦{x}^{2}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:x↦{x}^{2}-3$ means that the function $f$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to ${x}^{2}-3$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6068ace-0000-52ff-ba22-b00a6a6a4d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h: 5x ↦ x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171302} {:answer/id #uuid "c729352a-a733-50b1-b348-35c6af9d85d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h: x ↦ 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171303} {:answer/id #uuid "21914b42-fe24-5191-a1d5-5fffae8b7748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h: x ↦ 5x + x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171304} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab01e2e-c996-5129-827b-667f3aec11dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h: 5x ↦ x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171305} {:answer/id #uuid "7c7d4e90-bf21-569c-8590-c35331105267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h: 5x + x^{2} ↦ x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21914b42-fe24-5191-a1d5-5fffae8b7748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h: x ↦ 5x + x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171304} :answer-field/id #uuid "837f95dd-da0f-5f88-96cb-6acd0668567c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2423c376-50d4-5d9b-9b03-1de914522887" :question/math-academy-id "q-96043" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $h(x)=5x+{x}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $h(x)=5x+{x}^{2}$ means that the function $h$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $5x+{x}^{2}$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nh:x↦5x+{x}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7817d247-fcf1-50c7-9b63-9eb716a5c516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302334} {:answer/id #uuid "736ddfbe-1ed5-5d1e-a718-2a9e13a5996a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302335} {:answer/id #uuid "cf76de5f-1260-5e97-8e2c-8de2abb911cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302336} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e177de-562f-5ddb-bf33-dbe15f1c995c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302337} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cfa53f-bc23-534f-9f3b-33e7aff0d589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf76de5f-1260-5e97-8e2c-8de2abb911cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302336} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf9cb95-400a-5a38-abc6-a84b7fca1368" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e0de236-3312-5e3d-962b-5a388a311795" :question/math-academy-id "q-96741" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain ${-1,1}?$\n\n- $p:{-1,1}\\to R$\n- $q:R\\to [-1,1)$\n- $r:[-1,1)\\to {-1,1}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[-1,1),$ the interval from $-1$ to $1$ (including $-1$ but not including $1$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is ${-1,1},$ the set containing the two numbers $-1$ and $1$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b739880c-ec73-5a01-9008-1d48bce8aefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302341} {:answer/id #uuid "4f561fe7-0ccd-582b-91aa-8436c7371012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302342} {:answer/id #uuid "56531bc0-1108-5803-9c33-6252b8095882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302343} {:answer/id #uuid "97a6076b-51af-56c2-ab83-405722ca1198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302344} {:answer/id #uuid "d977fc8d-ff36-5d85-b825-69fe3dd01309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56531bc0-1108-5803-9c33-6252b8095882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302343} :answer-field/id #uuid "596e1808-c1f4-5bc3-9e92-2d2f5cb6cfd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "002be73b-0850-5dcf-98e6-fc20bcccc216" :question/math-academy-id "q-96745" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain ${1,2}?$\n\n- $p:{1,2}\\to R$\n- $q:R\\to [1,2)$\n- $r:[1,2)\\to {1,2}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is ${1,2},$ the set containing the two numbers $1$ and $2$ only.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $[1,2),$ the interval from $1$ to $2$ (including $1$ but not including $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b30a09be-9b64-5239-8239-f3aaf7fe8eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302348} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f4c6d7-9f38-52e7-81c2-1b86ac5f789a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302349} {:answer/id #uuid "1c4ededc-3f84-5f09-a5a7-4ae048764dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302350} {:answer/id #uuid "2b0e64c9-481e-5318-a793-8cb7067bf72a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302351} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e05644-182b-5626-9678-ff178a581349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3e05644-182b-5626-9678-ff178a581349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302352} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a7a63f4-2576-56fe-bc40-5f36c6a8df9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06651007-fef5-56e8-a70d-1438580a8df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-96748" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the domain $[2,4]?$\n\n- $p:{2,4}\\to R$\n- $r:[2,4]\\to {2,4}$\n- $q:R\\to [2,4]$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is ${2,4},$ the set containing the two numbers $2$ and $4$ only.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $[2,4],$ the interval from $2$ to $4$ (including $2$ and $4$).\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df67ba0a-4b43-53f0-9bdf-c272367ee29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302355} {:answer/id #uuid "03d79efc-c602-5870-9f6a-ad72010a0e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302356} {:answer/id #uuid "3f6a120a-62ff-599a-9577-58fee624934e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:Y\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302357} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cf0d57-57ae-5d89-910c-6b13b1dfec62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:Y\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302358} {:answer/id #uuid "cb582515-c081-5d16-9e87-97cd3d8f9913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:Q\\to Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df67ba0a-4b43-53f0-9bdf-c272367ee29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302355} :answer-field/id #uuid "af6b5e7d-f5a9-5349-a289-7ef6f6628e27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "017b78ef-16f0-5056-9e83-f2f23609a4ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-96752" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $f$ maps the set of real numbers to the set $Y.$\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $f$ maps the set of real numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $f:R\\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $f$ is $R,$ and the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32885433-3ff6-55fd-929d-4cf8cfa93713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Q\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302362} {:answer/id #uuid "86a0f1d6-b455-58ac-87b6-04e2e0682b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:[0,1]\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302363} {:answer/id #uuid "408e9659-9975-590b-92a7-8989d5bfe97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Q\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302364} {:answer/id #uuid "63bc92d9-b927-50ca-9c06-10f6e2abeac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:R\\to Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302365} {:answer/id #uuid "57f3b777-d1ba-55c1-bb0c-6b364645149e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:R\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "408e9659-9975-590b-92a7-8989d5bfe97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g:Q\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302364} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c460148-b14c-519b-93b9-48b692f533df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8741fc43-1918-5d52-9141-b25d4ba5dd79" :question/math-academy-id "q-96755" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set of real numbers.\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set of real numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:Q\\to R$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $Q,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cfb8db6-c2af-52a5-a041-8c3487d9d723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:{0,1}\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302369} {:answer/id #uuid "ca81f5c9-f737-50a4-8624-cfbf53b05880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to {0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302370} {:answer/id #uuid "84472d08-8400-5c0c-b2f9-860f8326e0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to (0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302371} {:answer/id #uuid "f914e1d2-07ae-52e1-ab15-f08afa87263f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:(0,1)\\to R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302372} {:answer/id #uuid "506a9025-5248-5c26-a114-ef6aff6891d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to [0,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca81f5c9-f737-50a4-8624-cfbf53b05880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f:R\\to {0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302370} :answer-field/id #uuid "25fd1641-c911-5bd4-bec0-cf41a8153bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b8ec985-406a-5e35-b3f2-08400d1b2234" :question/math-academy-id "q-96760" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $f$ maps the set of real numbers to the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $f$ maps the set of real numbers to the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $f:R\\to {0,1}$\n\nHere, the domain of $f$ is $R,$ and the codomain of $f$ is ${0,1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f135aa5c-c9c3-54a8-a4cf-55b898b8cd6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)=-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302376} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bdb18c-c284-5527-a4fb-c968a431d54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302377} {:answer/id #uuid "9738aa53-08b7-5d7b-a156-ee35d61c6cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)={x}^{2}-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302378} {:answer/id #uuid "76e70ea9-18a4-5114-ab22-7e90e893c673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302379} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d6b24e-e43e-5222-afe6-987181683a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={x}^{2}-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9738aa53-08b7-5d7b-a156-ee35d61c6cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)={x}^{2}-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302378} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ebba0e-a9c8-5045-a3ae-f8e311859c32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6f18196-a840-5507-b2f5-03dac9bdc39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-96820" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $h:x↦{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $h:x↦{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ means that the function $h$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to ${x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{2}-{x}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e8f7939-f58e-5229-9e1e-36a37281a319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302383} {:answer/id #uuid "59aff3bd-4d7e-52d8-9684-11b12ede5892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302384} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b26e1b-e89f-596c-a84b-5948596b7658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302385} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ab4f89-f408-5968-b91d-76ceeee573fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302386} {:answer/id #uuid "f18200a4-8b75-5967-8e15-99a45c49adae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0ab4f89-f408-5968-b91d-76ceeee573fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302386} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca6bae2-94ea-589e-b40b-d3c9ef07553c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4dc1b813-bafc-58ed-ba25-aa80143f8224" :question/math-academy-id "q-96826" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g:R\\to R?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is $R$\n- The codomain of $g$ is $R$\n- The range of $g$ is $R$" :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng:R\\to R\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $R$ to the set $R.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $R,$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $R.$ Note that this is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5745fd5-8156-56df-8d7d-864885bcf31e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302390} {:answer/id #uuid "67cce654-8dbb-5904-8096-a76ec86d18f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302391} {:answer/id #uuid "abc487d4-a22b-5459-92d4-8e71c11b2324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302392} {:answer/id #uuid "b9082e9a-b3f1-5566-b3d6-24aee8e1e532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302393} {:answer/id #uuid "ff77b335-132b-5e90-b3f0-b5543bf86e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abc487d4-a22b-5459-92d4-8e71c11b2324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302392} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc0440e9-4afb-5747-80b5-d169f37831ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ece4cad-7baa-5180-acf5-d91ffd5ed6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96830" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/assets/740d578e554cd61bc733079c32c063d41ab4d9dcc775d36f7080ee8e5aa5a622.png)\n\nConsider the function $f:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $f$ is ${5,10,15}$\n- The codomain of $f$ is ${5,10,12,13,14,15}$\n- The range of $f$ is ${12,13,14}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X={5,10,15},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y={12,13,14},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is ${12,13},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"I only.\""}]}
